JavaScript normally executes code one line at a time.

console.log("A");       
console.log("B");       
console.log("C");       

Output:
A
B
C

This is synchronous execution.

But some operations take time, such as:
Getting data from an API
Reading a file
Waiting for a timer
Database operations
Sending a request to a server

We don't want JavaScript to stop everything while waiting.
That's where asynchronous JavaScript comes in.



[Synchronous vs Asynchronous]

Synchronous:-
console.log("Start");                       
console.log("Doing something...");         
console.log("End");                        
Output:-
Start
Doing something...
End

Each statement waits for the previous one.

Asynchronous:-
console.log("Start");
setTimeout(() => {
    console.log("Doing something...");
}, 2000);
console.log("End");
Output:-
Start
End
Doing something...

Why setTimeout() tells JavaScript: Run this function after 2 seconds, but don't block the rest of the program.



[Callbacks]
A callback is simply a function passed to another function so that it can be executed later.
The callback runs after the asynchronous operation finishes. 

[Promise]
A Promise is a guarantee that you'll get a result in the future — either success or failure.
A Promise has 3 states:
Pending   →  waiting, not done yet
Fulfilled →  completed successfully ✅
Rejected  →  something went wrong   ❌

resolve() means: The operation finished successfully. Here is the result. → success
reject() means: Something went wrong.                                     → failure  

.then()	means: Handle successful Promise result (resolve)
.catch() means: Handle failure Promise error (reject)
.finally() means: Runs always, success or failure (resolve or reject)
